800|        /// it is on. The frame guard means that once the dispatcher calls PreSimulationInputTick again this
801|        /// method costs one int compare and returns - no double substep, no double publish.
802|        ///
803|        /// Late-frame is after the updatable walk, so in the degraded configuration consumers see the
804|        /// override one frame later than they would from pre-simulation. That is a one-frame latency on a
805|        /// lane that was previously producing nothing at all.
806|        /// </summary>
807|        private void PumpPreSimulationInputIfDispatcherSkipped()
808|        {
809|            if (!_isInitialized)
810|                return;
811|
812|            int currentFrame = Hecton8.Core.SystemDispatcher.CurrentFrameIndex;
813|            if (_lastPreSimulationInputFrame == currentFrame)
814|                return;
815|
816|            DiagRecordPumpFired();
817|            PreSimulationInputTick(Hecton8.Core.SystemDispatcher.CurrentFrameUnscaledDeltaTime);
818|        }
819|
820|        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
821|        // Automation-override hop census.
822|        //
823|        // WHY THIS EXISTS. Logs/h8_worldsim_probe5.log reports the Swim moment as
824|        // "movementIntent01max=0.000 ... inputServiceRegistered=True inputEnabled=True
825|        // switchToPlayerInputCalled=True blockMask=0x00000000", and the VERBSWEEP row as
826|        // "overrideFlagSeen=False overridesPublished=152 ... lastResolvedButtons=0x00000000 atFrame=0" plus
827|        // "LANECENSUS ... InputStateSignal=0". That is five numbers and they do NOT localise the fault,
828|        // because four of them are read from ONE field.
829|        //
830|        // H8_HeadlessWorldDriver.cs:1153-1157 samples overrideFlagSeen, atFrame, lastResolvedButtons and
831|        // arrivedInResolvedSnapshot all from IInputService.CurrentInputState, which is _currentInputState.
832|        // That field is assigned in exactly one place, PublishDeterministicInputState (:818), inside the try
833|        // block AFTER TryAcquireInputMutationGuard and the four TryResolveInputBuffer calls have all
834|        // succeeded, and publishInputState = true is set in the same straight-line block a few statements
835|        // later (:831). So "_currentInputState was updated" and "an InputStateSignal was pushed" are the same
836|        // event. InputStateSignal=0 therefore forces overrideFlagSeen=False, atFrame=0 and
837|        // lastResolvedButtons=0 REGARDLESS of whether the override lane was consumed. The driver's own hint
838|        // text - "false means TryConsumeLatestInputOverride never accepted the publish" - does not follow.
839|        //
840|        // Two hypotheses survive probe5 and produce byte-identical observables:
841|        //   H1 LateFrameTick() never ran, so the self-pump above never fired, so PreSimulationInputTick never
842|        //      ran. CaptureState is its only per-frame caller, so _currentState stayed at the all-zero cold
843|        //      capture (movementIntent01max=0.000) and nothing was ever published (InputStateSignal=0).
844|        //      Registration is a live suspect: TryRegisterToDispatcher (:2878) is the sole writer of
845|        //      _registeredLateFrame, it returns early when GlobalRegistry.Dispatcher is null, its result is
846|        //      never retried, and a false return is not logged anywhere.
847|        //   H2 PreSimulationInputTick DID run, but every TryConsumeLatestInputOverride hit the age branch at
848|        //      CoreDeterminismSignals.cs:197-202, which clears the signal and returns false with no log at
849|        //      all - and, independently, the publish gate failed, because
850|        //      OpenOrAcquireInputBufferForOwnerRoute (:1358) returns false on IsAllocationLocked /
851|        //      IsCompactionFenceActive and TryAcquireInputMutationGuard (:1418) returns false off the owner
852|        //      thread, both silently. probe5 does show vault turbulence across the menu transition
853|        //      (FatalMemoryLeakException at 01_MAIN_MENU).
854|        // The clock-skew LogError at CoreDeterminismSignals.cs:212 fired ZERO times in probe5, which rules
855|        // out the frame-clock latch fixed in 1261c9fc6 but does not separate H1 from H2.
856|        //
857|        // The census below separates them in one run. Counters are printed in pipeline order, so the first
858|        // zero from the left is the break point: lateFrameTick=0 is H1; lateFrameTick>0 with publishOk=0 and
859|        // a nonzero publishGuardFail/publishBufferFail is H2's publish gate; overrideRejected>0 with
860|        // overrideApplied=0 is the consume gate.
861|        //
862|        // It is emitted from a READ accessor, not from a tick lane, and that is deliberate. Every tick lane
863|        // this component owns (LateFrameTick, SlowTick) is registered by the same TryRegisterToDispatcher
864|        // call, so under H1 all of them are dead and a census emitted from one of them would print nothing -
865|        // the useless outcome. probe5 PROVES the driver read CurrentInputState (it printed atFrame and
866|        // lastResolvedButtons from it), so that accessor is the one observation point in this file with
867|        // measured reachability in the failing configuration.
868|        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
869|        private void DiagRecordPumpFired()
870|        {